# {{ .name }}

.NET loader integration. An ASP.NET service subscribes to the `{{ .topic }}`
topic of the in-memory pub/sub: the Dapr sidecar POSTs each delivered message
to the subscription endpoint, which runs it through the Intropy loader
pipeline and writes the result as `{orderId}.json` through the `destination`
folder binding. The loader is a long-running service (a Deployment in
production) and keeps running until you stop it.

## Run

```bash
task run
```

This starts the local stack (grafana/otel-lgtm observability plus the Intropy
Idempotency Service and Business Incident Service — see
`local/platform-services/`) and runs the app under Dapr, listening on port
5001. At startup the sidecar calls `GET /dapr/subscribe`, discovers the
subscription endpoint, and from then on POSTs every delivery to it. Check the
service is up:

```bash
curl http://localhost:5001/healthz
```

A subscriber alone has nothing publishing to it. The in-memory pub/sub lives
inside this integration's own sidecar, so a standalone loader is verified by
publishing through that sidecar — which is exactly what `--publish-app-id`
does. In a second terminal:

```bash
dapr publish --publish-app-id {{ .appId }} --pubsub pubsub --topic {{ .topic }} \
  --data '{"orderId":"ORD-2001","customerId":"CUST-42","orderDate":"2026-07-24T09:30:00Z","lines":[{"sku":"SKU-AAA","quantity":1,"unitPrice":12.50}]}'
```

(`task publish` runs the same command.) The loader picks it up:

```
== APP == info: {{ .name }}.Pipeline[0]
== APP ==       Loaded ORD-2001 from topic {{ .topic }} (pubsub)
```

and writes the destination file:

```bash
cat local/storage/{{ .connector }}/ORD-2001.json
```

{{- if not .empty }}

Publish the same order again and the pipeline cancels at the idempotency
check instead of loading twice — the log shows
`Cancelled ORD-2001 as a duplicate (idempotency check)` and no file is
rewritten.
{{- end }}

Other tasks: `task build`, `task test`, `task clean` (remove
`./local/storage`), and `task services:down` (stop the local stack). See
`Taskfile.yml`.

## Shared contracts

The canonical contract types (`Order`, `OrderLine`) live in the sibling
`../{{ .contracts }}` project, referenced from `src/{{ .name }}.csproj`. It was
scaffolded together with this project (or already existed — components in the
same parent directory share it); the publishing extractor serializes exactly
those records. Only the destination load record (`Out`) stays local in
`src/Model/`.

## Container image

```bash
docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .
```

Multi-stage build on the .NET 10 chiseled runtime. The shared contracts sibling
enters the build as the `contracts` named context (it lies outside this build
context). The image contains only the app — the Dapr sidecar and components
come from the deployment environment.

## Pipeline

Per delivered message (`src/Endpoints/LoaderEndpoints.cs` rebuilds the
envelope into a CloudEvent and drives this):
{{- if .empty }}

1. `Deserializer` parses `CloudEvent.Data` into the shared `Order` contract.
2. `Validator` enforces business rules.
3. Idempotency check — cancels duplicates.
4. `Transformer` maps `Order` → `Out`.
5. `Sender` writes `Out` through the `destination` binding.

Every step body throws `NotImplementedException` — this project was scaffolded
with empty bodies for a migration agent to fill in. The idempotency and
business-incident wiring is already in place; the idempotency step keys off
CloudEvent Subject/Time from the context, so backfill those from stable
business fields in your `Deserializer` (see `AGENTS.md`).
{{- else }}

1. `Deserializer` parses `CloudEvent.Data` into the shared `Order` contract
   (from `../{{ .contracts }}` — the same record the extractor publishes), stamps
   `order_id` into the pipeline context, and stamps the `subject`/`time`
   context keys from the order's `OrderId`/`OrderDate` — for
   extractor-published events these equal the envelope values, and a
   `dapr publish` envelope carries no subject and a wall-clock time.
2. `Validator` enforces required fields and basic business rules; failures are
   routed to the Business Incident Service.
3. Idempotency check — asks the Idempotency Service whether this Subject /
   Time combination was already processed; duplicates cancel the run.
4. `Transformer` maps the canonical order to the load record `Out`
   (`LoadedAt` = now, `Status` = `"Loaded"`).
5. `Sender` serializes `Out` with default System.Text.Json (PascalCase) and
   writes `{orderId}.json` through the `destination` binding.

Success, an idempotent duplicate, and a routed business incident all
acknowledge the message (`status: SUCCESS` in the response body); a technical
failure returns `status: RETRY` and the sidecar redelivers.
{{- end }}
